  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Melanoplus tribulus, commonly known as the Tribulus Short-wing Grasshopper, is a member of the Acrididae family (Short-horned Grasshoppers) native to New England. This small to medium-sized grasshopper is characterized by its reduced wing length and robust build adapted to northeastern habitats.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Short Wings",
      "description": "The wings are noticeably shortened (brachypterous), not extending to the abdomen's tip, distinguishing it from fully-winged species",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Body Coloration",
      "description": "Typically displays brownish or grayish coloration with darker markings on the head and thorax",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Hind Leg Structure",
      "description": "Robust hind femurs with distinctive banding patterns and powerful muscles for jumping",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "M. tribulus inhabits dry grasslands, meadows, and disturbed areas throughout New England, preferring open sunny locations with sparse vegetation.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Short-winged grasshoppers like M. tribulus rely primarily on jumping for escape rather than flight, making them well-adapted to ground-level habitats where wings would be disadvantageous.",
